    "summary": "High‑energy set with soaring 'Comes A Time' and tight rockers",
    "blurb": "The show bursts with a standout vocal performance on \"Comes A Time\" and crisp, rocking renditions of \"Jack Straw\", \"Tennessee Jed\" and the Not Fade Away > Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad medley. Tight transitions and spirited interplay keep the momentum flowing throughout both sets.",
    "review": "The October 30, 1971 Taft Auditorium gig captures the Dead in a joyous, high‑octane mood as they roll through two solid sets and an encore. Jerry’s voice is on fire for \"Comes A Time\", delivering an emotive falsetto that resonates over Keith Godchaux’s bright, rock‑in’ piano debut. Phil lays down wonderful bass lines – his signature “bombs” add extra punch – while Bob blends tight rhythm chops with strong harmonies on tunes like \"Me And Bobby McGee\" and \"Jack Straw\". The second set shines with a seamless Not Fade Away > Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad sequence, and the band rides a breezy groove into a rousing \"Truckin'\" that feels both relaxed and propulsive. Though one audience‑sourced mix buries Jerry’s guitar, the excellent soundboard capture (prefm.nawrocki) offers crystal‑clear clarity, making this show a rewarding listen for anyone wanting a taste of the early‑’70s Dead in full swing.",
